-- title:
--   Lemma EC.9 — the hedged optimal VPQ is at most $R_1=R^{NV(M)}(P_1)$
-- statement:
--   Throughout, the standing model of §3.1 holds (the published `HedgePSNV.Model.Standing`): $B,\tilde B$ independent Brownian motions with augmented filtrations $\mathcal F\supseteq\mathcal G$, the asset $dX_t=X_t(\mu_t\,dt+\sigma_t\,dB_t)$, the demand component $C$ with $A_T=C_T+\tilde\sigma\tilde B_T$, the two Markov hypotheses, and $\mathbb P^M$ with $d\mathbb P^M/d\mathbb P=Z_T$ (12); $V_0(P,R)=\mathbb E^M[H_T(P,R)]$, and $D=\{(P,R):P\ge c,\ R-bP\ge0\}$.
--
--   In the setting of §EC.8.3.1 — Assumptions 1–5 (Assumption 1 under $\mathbb P$ and $\mathbb P^M$; Assumptions 2–3 at a maximizer $(P^{NV},R^{NV})$ of problem (2)), $C_T\preceq C^M_T$, $m=\mathbb E[H_T(P^{NV},R^{NV})]$ and $\mathbb E[((C_T)^-)^2]<\infty$ — let $P_1$ be the smallest solution of $V_0(P_1,R^{NV(M)}(P_1))=m$ and $R_1=R^{NV(M)}(P_1)$ (Definition EC.1(i)). Then every minimizer $(P^h_m,R^h_m)$ of $B(m,\cdot,\cdot)$ over $D$ satisfies
--   $$
--   R^h_m\le R_1 ,
--   $$
--   for every section maximizer $R_1$ of $V_0(P_1,\cdot)$.
--
--   It reduces Theorem 3(ii)–(iii) to bounds on $R_1$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: $\mathcal F_t$ and $\mathcal G_t$ are the filtrations generated by $(B_t,\tilde B_t)$ and $B_t$ (p.11) → the $\mathbb P$-augmented natural filtrations of $(B,\tilde B)$ and of $B$ (published `HedgePSNV.Model.Standing`); reason: martingale representation (Prop. EC.3) holds for the augmented Brownian filtration, and null-set modifications of an adapted process must stay adapted
--   - reading: $(X_t,\mu_t,\sigma_t)$ is Markovian under $\mathbb P$ and $(X_t,\sigma_t)$ is Markovian under $\mathbb P^M$ (pp.11–12) → the simple Markov property on $[0,T]$ with respect to $\mathcal F$ (`IsMarkovWrt`); reason: the paper does not define the term; this is the standard definition, relative to the filtration of footnote 4
--   - encoding: processes on $[0,T]$; stochastic integrals with almost-sure integrability → processes indexed by $\mathbb R_{\ge0}$, constrained for $t\le T$; the published relation `EthierKurtz.HasBrownianItoIntegral` plus a pathwise drift integral; reason: values after $T$ never enter; under the augmented filtration the integrand classes agree up to indistinguishability (published base convention)
--   - reading: $c$ the unit production cost, $s$ the unit salvage value, $X_0$ an asset price → $s<c$ and $x_0>0$ in the published parameter structure; reason: the newsvendor convention (p.7), implied by Assumption 3 wherever it is assumed; $X_t>0$ is used on p.ec28
--   - reading: $(P^{NV},R^{NV})$ the solution of problem (2), named by Assumptions 2–3 → any maximizer of $\mathbb E[H_T]$ over $D$ at which Assumptions 2 and 3 hold; reason: uniqueness is the content of Proposition 1 and is not built into a definition
--   - reading: Assumption 1 on the distribution of the market size $A_T$ → Assumption 1 for the law of $A_T$ under $\mathbb P$ and for its law under $\mathbb P^M$; reason: the paper applies the newsvendor analysis under both measures (Table EC.3; proofs on pp.ec49–ec57 use $F_M$ and $f_M$), as in the published base items
--   - encoding: $C_T\preceq C^M_T$, with $C^M_T$ the $\mathbb P$-version of the $\mathbb P^M$-law of $C_T$ and $X\preceq Y$ iff $\mathbb P(Y\le a)\le\mathbb P(X\le a)$ (p.12) → `UsualOrder P P^M C_T C_T`: $\mathbb P(C_T>x)\le\mathbb P^M(C_T>x)$ for all $x$; reason: the law of $C^M_T$ under $\mathbb P$ is the law of $C_T$ under $\mathbb P^M$; for probability measures the tail form is the distribution-function form
--   - reading: $P^{NV(M)}(R):=\arg\max_PV_0(P,R)$, $R^{NV(M)}(P):=\arg\max_RV_0(P,R)$ (EC.8.2), (EC.8.9) → every maximizer over the section of $D$ (`IsSecMaxP`, `IsSecMaxR`); reason: the convention of the published Lemma EC.7: with the unconstrained formula $R^{NV(M)}(c)=F_M^{-1}(0)=-\infty$; the closed forms are the section maximizers wherever they lie in the section
--   - reading: $(P^h_m,R^h_m):=\arg\min_{P,R}B(m,P,R)$ (15) → every minimizer $(P,R)$ of $B(m,\cdot,\cdot)$ over $D$; no minimizer is asserted to exist; reason: the stronger of the two readings; the paper's arguments are comparison arguments valid at any minimizer
--   - reading: Lemma EC.9 prints no hypotheses (§EC.8.3.1, inside the proof of Theorem 3) → Theorem 3's hypotheses with $m=\mathbb E[H_T(P^{NV},R^{NV})]$; reason: the lemma is stated in that setting and its proof uses Lemma EC.7 and the definition of $P_1$
--   - encoding: the mean–variance problems (13)–(14) presuppose $\mathrm{Var}[H_T(P,R)]<\infty$ (p.9, p.ec23) → the hypothesis $\mathbb E[((C_T)^-)^2]<\infty$ (`NegPartL2`); reason: with the Gaussian term $\tilde\sigma\tilde B_T$ it is equivalent to $H_T(P,R)\in L^2(\mathbb P)$ for all $(P,R)$; it excludes only the case where problem (14) is undefined
--   - encoding: $B(m,P,R)$ the minimum of a variance over admissible hedges with mean $m$ (14) → the published `Bval`, an infimum in $[0,\infty]$ of the extended variance; reason: an empty feasible set gives $+\infty$, not a junk $0$
--   - encoding: an admissible strategy $\vartheta=\{\theta_t\}$ with $\chi_t(\vartheta)=\int_0^t\theta_sdX_s$ (EC.7.15) → a pair $(\theta,\chi)$ with $\chi$ an $\mathcal F$-adapted version of the gains on $[0,T]$; integrands progressively measurable; reason: the stochastic integral is fixed only up to null sets, so the strategy is carried with its gains (published `AX`)
-- source:
--   Wang–Yao–Zhang, How Does Risk Hedging Impact Operations? Insights from a Price-Setting Newsvendor Model, arXiv:2201.01026v4, Lemma EC.9, p.ec52 (PDF 88); Definition EC.1, p.ec50 (PDF 86)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_HedgePSNV_Hedging
import Definitions.Def_HedgePSNV_Supplement_SectionMax
import Definitions.Def_StochasticOrders_Usual_UsualOrder

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open scoped NNReal ENNReal

namespace HedgePSNV.Supplement

open HedgePSNV

/-- Lemma EC.9, arXiv:2201.01026v4, p.ec52, in the setting of §EC.8.3.1 (p.ec50: Assumptions
1–5, `C_T ⪯ C^M_T`, `m = E[H_T(P^NV, R^NV)]`): `R^h_m ≤ R1`, where `R1 := R^NV(M)(P1)` with `P1`
the smallest solution of `E^M[H_T(P1, R^NV(M)(P1))] = m` (Definition EC.1(i), p.ec50).

Precisely: for every minimizer `(P, R)` of `B(m, ·, ·)` over the decision domain, every least
element `P1` of `{P' : V_0(P', R') = m for some section maximizer R' of V_0(P', ·)}` and every
section maximizer `R1` of `V_0(P1, ·)`, `R ≤ R1`.

**Formalization Note** Section maximizers are over the sections of the decision domain, as in
the published Lemma EC.7. No finiteness of `B` is assumed: at this `m`, the zero hedge makes
`B(m, P^NV, R^NV)` finite, so every minimizer has finite `B`. `NegPartL2` is the
finite-variance presupposition of problem (14). `C_T ⪯ C^M_T` is
`UsualOrder P P^M C_T C_T`. -/
theorem lemma_ec_9 (p : Params) {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (M : Model p Ω)
    (hM : M.Standing) (hL2 : M.NegPartL2) (h4 : M.Assumption4) (h5 : M.Assumption5)
    (h1P : Assumption1 (M.P.map M.AT)) (h1M : Assumption1 (M.PM.map M.AT))
    (PNV RNV : ℝ) (hNV : IsNVMaximizer p M.P M.AT PNV RNV)
    (h2 : Assumption2 p M.P M.AT PNV RNV) (h3 : Assumption3 p M.P M.AT PNV RNV)
    (hneg : StochasticOrders.Usual.UsualOrder M.P M.PM (M.C p.T) (M.C p.T))
    (P R : ℝ) (hPR : (P, R) ∈ decisionDomain p)
    (hmin : ∀ P' R' : ℝ, (P', R') ∈ decisionDomain p →
      M.Bval (nvValue p M.P M.AT PNV RNV) P R ≤ M.Bval (nvValue p M.P M.AT PNV RNV) P' R')
    (P1 : ℝ) (hP1 : IsLeast {P' : ℝ | ∃ R' : ℝ, IsSecMaxR M P' R' ∧
        M.V0 P' R' = nvValue p M.P M.AT PNV RNV} P1)
    (R1 : ℝ) (hR1 : IsSecMaxR M P1 R1) :
    R ≤ R1 := by sorry

end HedgePSNV.Supplement
